# Infrastructure Economist Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Author:** Infrastructure economist (Stage 1, independent lens)
**Run:** quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2
**Date:** 2026-08-07

The question in front of MWAA is not whether a quiet-by-design standard is nice
to have. It is whether the acoustic, PA-zoning, mass-notification, and
information-display performance of new and substantially renovated terminals at
IAD and DCA will be set before the design freezes or after — and who pays the
difference. Every airport capital economist I have worked with will tell you
the same thing: the difference is not a rounding error, but the standard
itself is. That asymmetry is the entire economic argument for acting now.

## 1. Key findings

1. **MWAA is committing to a program whose scale dwarfs the cost of any
   defensible acoustic and PA standard, while its own rate base is already
   tightening.** MWAA's 2025 Official Statement forecasts cost per enplanement
   (CPE) rising to $20.71 by 2030 *before* the July 29, 2026 Dulles program is
   layered on [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. The
   announced Dulles transformation — "more than $20 billion," reported at
   $22.5 billion in press accounts — is roughly three times the $7 billion
   Dulles allocation the Board previously endorsed [Source: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/americas-newest-capital-gateway-unveiled-more-than-20-billion-transformation-at-washington-dulles-international-airport-302838221.html].
   Against a program that size, a fully specified acoustic and
   audio-communication standard is a fraction of one percent of program cost —
   trivial as CapEx, disproportionate as risk relief.

2. **The historical failure mode on airport megaprograms is precisely the
   family of systems a quiet-by-design standard governs.** Denver's automated
   baggage system opened 16 months late with an estimated $560M program
   overrun and a system that, by the time DIA opened in 1995, served one
   airline, one concourse, one direction [Source: https://www5.in.tum.de/persons/huckle/Drexel_DIA.htm].
   Heathrow Terminal 5 was built for £4.3B and then lost roughly 15,000 bags
   and cancelled ~300 flights inside its first weeks because the baggage and
   IT stack had not been commissioned to real-world load [Source: https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/wbna23117153]. Berlin
   Brandenburg's opening slipped nine years, in significant part because fire
   suppression, smoke control, and ventilation — the same
   life-safety/communication stack governed by NFPA-72-adjacent codes — were
   found to be non-compliant during commissioning [Source: https://www.airportwatch.org.uk/2020/10/nine-years-late-and-x3-over-budget-due-to-problems-berlins-brandenburg-airport-finally-opens-during-a-pandemic/].
   Systems that carry safety announcements, wayfinding, and passenger
   information are the ones that most often fail commissioning.

3. **US airport demand for capital is not slackening.** The FAA's NPIAS
   2025–2029 identified approximately $67.5B in AIP- and BIL-eligible airport
   development needs across ~18,100 projects at 3,287 airports — an 8%
   increase over the prior report's $62.4B [Source: https://acconline.org/industry-news-new-faa-npias-identifies-over-62-billion-in-aip-eligible-needs/].
   ACI World projects roughly $2.4T in global airport capital investment to
   2040, with about $400B (17%) in North America [Source: https://blog.aci.aero/airport-capacity/airports-need-significant-capital-expenditure-to-alleviate-slot-constraints/].
   The industry-wide implication: the marginal capital dollar must earn its
   keep. Standards that reduce late-stage change orders on communication
   systems are among the cheapest yield the industry has.

4. **The MacLeamy cost-of-change curve is the underlying economic mechanism.**
   Formalized by Patrick MacLeamy (then at HOK) in 2004, the curve shows that
   the ability to influence cost falls, and the cost of change rises,
   monotonically from predesign through construction documentation to
   construction [Source: https://www.danieldavis.com/macleamy/]. Airport
   experience is consistent with the general construction-industry finding
   that change orders during construction cost multiples of what the same
   change would cost during schematic design. For acoustic geometry (ceiling
   volume, hard-surface area, RT60), PA loudspeaker density and zoning, and
   MNS pathways, the "keystrokes-not-change-orders" window closes at
   60–90% design. That window is where a quiet-by-design standard has to
   land.

5. **The technical performance target itself is close to already-required
   code, not aspirational.** NFPA 72 Chapter 24 governs emergency
   communications-system voice intelligibility in life-safety applications,
   and the IEC 60268-16 Voice Alarm (VA) target of STI = 0.50 is the
   industry-recognized minimum for intelligible speech in transportation
   assembly spaces [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5].
   ACRP Research Report 175, *Improving Intelligibility of Airport Terminal
   Public Address Systems*, provides the specific airport-terminal design
   guidance [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/2018Annual-bit-session9-Glickman.pdf].
   Recent airport-terminal research recommends STI ≥ 0.45 for pier-style
   departure lounges as the minimum to satisfy ISO 9921 / IEC 60268-16 for
   safety-critical announcements [Source: https://doi.org/10.3390/buildings15030426].
   A quiet-by-design standard that specifies STI, RT60, and background-noise
   criteria is not asking for more than code and consensus already require —
   it is asking for it to be measured at commissioning rather than left to
   chance.

6. **IAD and DCA are two entirely different economic problems and a single
   uniform standard will misallocate.** IAD posted ~29.0M passengers in
   calendar 2025 (all-time record, 10.5M international) [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/financial-and-governance]
   and enters a decade-long program adding ~5M sf of new or renovated space
   [Source: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/americas-newest-capital-gateway-unveiled-more-than-20-billion-transformation-at-washington-dulles-international-airport-302838221.html].
   DCA posted ~24.9M passengers in 2025, down 5.3% from 26.3M in 2024 — its
   lowest count since 2022 — and operates within a historic-district Terminal
   1 and a Pelli-designed Terminal 2 whose retrofit envelope is small [context
   packet §6, §5.2]. A quiet-by-design standard that is mandatory only at the
   floor (STI, RT60, background noise, MNS intelligibility) and tiered above
   that (PA zoning granularity, quiet-zone provisioning, dynamic
   announcement-content control) is the only economically defensible shape.
   Anything else either overspends at DCA or undershoots at IAD.

7. **Even conservatively sized, the standard is an infinitesimal share of
   the program and a real share of downside protection.** Concourse-scale
   US construction is running from roughly $50/sf to well over $1,000/sf
   depending on program complexity; JAX Concourse B is at approximately
   $1,178/sf for a $220M six-gate project [Source: https://www.jaxdailyrecord.com/news/2024/may/09/220-million-permit-means-concourse-b-airport-construction-can-take-off/].
   In that context, incremental acoustical treatment, loudspeaker density,
   zoning-controller channel counts, and FIDS density are almost always
   sub-single-digit-percent line items. The economic question is not whether
   MWAA can afford the standard; it is whether MWAA can afford *not* to
   commission against one. The Heathrow, Denver, and BER precedents say no.

8. **Opportunity cost is real but it cuts toward, not away from, adopting
   the standard.** The Bond Buyer reporting on the Dulles announcement quotes
   industry analyst Edward Russell projecting a $90 CPE could ultimately be
   required to finance the program [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions] —
   an outer-bound figure worth caveating, but a directional signal
   nonetheless. Airline signatories will look at every dollar in that stack.
   Preventing the kind of post-open PA-and-baggage debacle that cost British
   Airways ~£16M in a single event at T5 in 2008 (and again at T5 in May
   2026, with BA reportedly demanding up to £10M from Heathrow for a single
   20,000-bag failure) [Source: https://www.nomadlawyer.org/heathrow-baggage-failure-travel-chaos-british-airways-demands-compensation]
   is a cost avoidance that is easier to sell to signatories than most.

## 2. Evidence and analysis

### 2.1 The MWAA financial envelope

MWAA carries approximately $4.9B in outstanding revenue-bond debt as of July
2025 disclosures [context packet §3]. Its most recent primary-market issuance
was $714.4M in June 2025, with Aa3/AA- AMT bonds pricing at ~4.37% for the
2036 maturity [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/images/OS/MWAA%202025A%20OS.pdf; context §3].
The FY2025 signatory CPE was $9.56 at IAD and $7.49 at DCA per the ACFR, with
2026 first-half rate-book figures of $12.77 at IAD and $10.41 at DCA
[Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions].
By 2030 the pre-transformation projection is $20.71 CPE; the transformation
layers on top of that. This matters for the quiet-by-design question because
airline consent to premises-affecting standards runs through the same
use-and-lease framework that governs the rate base — the 15-year use agreement
adopted in 2024 and effective January 1, 2025 [context §4]. Standards that are
specified early, priced into the program brief, and consulted through the MII
process are far less likely to become airline-consent flashpoints than
retrofit specifications that arrive after tenants have signed off on gate
layouts.

### 2.2 The MacLeamy mechanics, in airport context

The MacLeamy curve is not an airport concept, but it applies with unusual
force in terminal work because so many airport systems (PA, MNS, FIDS, tenant
IT, airline paging, security-checkpoint messaging, gate-hold-room audio) are
overlapping tenants of the same physical volumes. Change one and you touch
others. The curve's basic finding — that design decisions in schematic and
design-development are 5–10x cheaper than the same decisions during
construction documentation, and 20–100x cheaper than during construction
[Source: https://www.danieldavis.com/macleamy/] — maps directly to the
economics of specifying loudspeaker density, ceiling absorptive treatment,
partition acoustics, and control-system zoning. IAD's Concourse E — 435,000
sf, 14 gates, scheduled to open in September 2026 with HOK designing the
future Tier 2/3 concourses — sits at exactly the design phase where
quiet-by-design specifications are cheap [context §5.1]. For future concourses
that will begin schematic design in 2027–2029, the window is now.

### 2.3 The historical failure record

The cases the operator asked me to weigh — Berlin Brandenburg, LAX APM,
Heathrow T5, Denver baggage, JFK T1, IAD AeroTrain — are worth reading as a
single pattern rather than six anecdotes.

- **Berlin Brandenburg (BER):** planned 2011 open, actually opened October
  2020. Reported cost escalation from an initial €2.4B budget to a
  €6.5B–€7.3B final cost (~2.3–2.6x), with some sources including interest
  and reaching ~€10B [Source: https://www.airportwatch.org.uk/2020/10/nine-years-late-and-x3-over-budget-due-to-problems-berlins-brandenburg-airport-finally-opens-during-a-pandemic/].
  Root cause of the biggest slips: fire suppression, smoke evacuation, and
  emergency-communication systems failing certification [Source: https://erena.me/2025/01/28/german-construction-fiasco/].
- **Denver International (DIA) baggage:** $186M system, part of a program
  whose overrun is commonly estimated at ~$560M, and a 16-month opening
  delay costing the city ~$1M/day [Source: https://www5.in.tum.de/persons/huckle/Drexel_DIA.htm].
  Scope was expanded mid-project from one airline to all airlines without
  commensurate risk analysis. This is the archetype of "specification drift
  after design freeze."
- **Heathrow Terminal 5:** built for £4.3B, opened March 2008; within days,
  ~300 flights cancelled and ~15,000 bags separated from passengers due to
  baggage system and staff-familiarization issues [Source: https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/wbna23117153].
  Recurrence in May 2026: ~20,000 bags failed to make outbound flights and
  BA demanded up to £10M in compensation [Source: https://www.nomadlawyer.org/heathrow-baggage-failure-travel-chaos-british-airways-demands-compensation].
- **LAX Automated People Mover:** original $1.9B budget; costs escalated
  ~$880M, reaching roughly $3.34B after a 2024 settlement; opening slipped
  from 2023 into 2026 with contractor–owner litigation [Source: https://laist.com/news/transportation/lax-people-mover-delay-grand-jury-report].
- **JFK New Terminal One:** $9.5B project; November 2026 opening after a
  slip from June 2026; Moody's downgraded the JFK NTO LLC outlook from
  stable to negative citing construction delays; total borrowings above $6B
  [Source: https://simpleflying.com/6-billion-debt-clock-ticking-new-york-jfk-delayed-new-terminal-1/].
- **IAD AeroTrain:** opened January 2010 at $1.5B, left Concourse D
  without a station for the 16 years since — a stranded-asset outcome that
  the July 2026 program is now trying to unwind at a reported ~$3.75B
  extension cost [Source: https://airportimprovement.com/article/dulles-debuts-15-billion-train-system/;
  https://www.travelerstoday.com/articles/60677/20260801/dulles-airport-ditches-people-movers-225-billion-overhaul-fares-may-rise.htm].

The pattern is not "big airport projects overrun." It is "the systems that
carry information — bags, passengers, voice, alarm — are where design freezes
break." A quiet-by-design standard governs three of four of those systems.

### 2.4 The technical floor is cheap

ACRP Research Report 175 exists precisely because the industry has repeatedly
found that PA intelligibility is not achievable without integrated acoustic
and electronic design [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/2018Annual-bit-session9-Glickman.pdf].
The IEC 60268-16 Voice Alarm target of STI ≥ 0.50, and the newer
airport-specific research recommending STI ≥ 0.45 in pier-style departure
lounges [Source: https://doi.org/10.3390/buildings15030426], are performance
metrics that a design team can be held to at commissioning. NFPA 72 already
requires voice-evacuation intelligibility in occupancies MWAA operates. The
economic contribution of the quiet-by-design standard is not raising the
substantive bar so much as *making the existing bar buildable* — by requiring
that ceiling acoustics, PA zoning, and background noise be co-designed rather
than sequentially value-engineered.

## 3. Caveats and limitations

- **No public MWAA design-manual acoustic or PA specification was retrieved
  at stage zero** [context §9]. If MWAA already has a superseded internal
  standard, some findings here understate the baseline.
- **The $22.5B and $20B+ Dulles figures are announcement-stage.** Financing
  plan, board resolutions amending the CCP, and airline-consent posture are
  not yet publicly documented [context §5.1, §10]. Treat CPE projections
  built on those numbers as directional, not authoritative.
- **Cost-per-square-foot benchmarks for terminals span an order of
  magnitude** and are not comparable across regions and program types. The
  JAX $1,178/sf reference is one data point, not a benchmark; per-sf claims
  about acoustic subsystem cost should be built up bottom-up in RFP
  documents.
- **The MacLeamy curve is a graphical intuition, not a validated empirical
  distribution.** The airport-specific multiplier of design-vs-construction
  change cost has not been systematically measured in the public literature.
  Treat it as directional evidence of a real mechanism, not a coefficient.
- **ACRP Report 175 is authoritative for PA-intelligibility guidance but is
  not a code-adopted standard.** NFPA 72 Ch. 24 and IEC 60268-16 are the
  enforceable references.
- **Comparability of BER, DIA, LAX APM, and T5 to a US authority's terminal
  fit-out is limited.** These cases show what happens when
  information-carrying systems fail commissioning; they do not translate
  directly into $/sf or schedule days.

