## 5. Bottom line

The economic case for a MWAA quiet-by-design standard is not that quiet is
valuable in itself — that is an operations and passenger-experience case,
which my colleagues will make. The economic case is that the airport
capital record shows information-carrying systems (bags, voice, alarm) are
where large programs fail commissioning, and that the marginal cost of
specifying performance for those systems collapses if it is done before
schematic freeze and explodes if it is done after. With $20B+ moving into
design at IAD and a small but real retrofit envelope at DCA, MWAA's window
to spend a few million dollars specifying against a defensible floor — and
to save many multiples of that in change orders, litigation, and
airline-consent friction — is now.
